    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between water-based and solvent-based paver sealer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Both are usually acrylic; the difference is the carrier. Solvent-based dissolves the resin in xylene or similar, which carries it into the paver surface for a deeper, richer colour. Water-based suspends acrylic particles in water which coalesce into a film as the water evaporates, staying mostly on the surface."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which lasts longer, water-based or solvent-based paver sealer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Solvent-based typically by about a year per application, partly because some of the resin sits within the surface where abrasion reaches it less. The gap has narrowed considerably as water-based formulations have improved, and solvent's bigger long-term advantage is that recoats merge rather than building up."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I put water-based sealer over solvent-based?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. The emulsion will not bond properly to a cured solvent-based acrylic surface, and the result is peeling and patchy adhesion. This is the least forgiving combination in the whole subject. To change type you must strip the existing sealer completely and let the pavers dry fully first."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does solvent-based paver sealer look better?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For maximum wet look, generally yes, and the difference is most obvious on dark or textured stone. Because the solvent carries resin into the paver rather than leaving it all on top, the colour change reads as depth rather than surface gloss. Modern water-based wet-look products have closed much of the gap."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is water-based paver sealer easier to apply?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Considerably. It tolerates a heavy patch far better, because the thinner film releases water readily and a generous coat usually just looks slightly glossier rather than blushing white. It is fussier about temperature and humidity though, since the acrylic particles need the right conditions to coalesce into a sound film."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I tell which sealer is already on my pavers?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Dab a little xylene on an inconspicuous sealed area. If the film softens or becomes tacky, it is solvent-based acrylic. If it is unaffected, it is likely water-based or another chemistry. Either way, test your intended product on a hidden square foot and let it cure several days before committing the whole patio."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is solvent-based paver sealer safe to use?",
