    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The resin doing the sealing is often the same in both. The carrier is the variable, and it changes almost everything about the experience of applying it and the result you get.
        </p>

        <div id="same-resin" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Same Resin, a Different Carrier
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most paver sealers on both sides of this comparison are acrylic. What differs is what the acrylic is dissolved or suspended in before it reaches the paver, and how that carrier behaves as it leaves.
          </p>

          <p>
            A solvent-based sealer dissolves the acrylic in an organic solvent, commonly xylene. The solvent genuinely dissolves the resin into a solution, and it also softens and penetrates the paver surface as it carries the resin in. When the solvent evaporates, the resin is left partly within the surface and partly as a film on it, fused into a continuous layer.
          </p>

          <p>
            A water-based sealer suspends acrylic particles in water as an emulsion rather than dissolving them. The particles do not dissolve; they sit dispersed. As the water evaporates, the particles come together and coalesce into a film. That process is called coalescence and it is why water-based products are fussier about temperature and humidity: the particles have to fuse properly, and if conditions prevent it the film never reaches full integrity.
          </p>

          <p>
            That difference in mechanism drives every practical difference below. Solvent carries resin into the surface and re-dissolves anything already there. Water leaves particles on the surface that must fuse together.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
