        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Both are acrylic; the carrier is what differs. Solvent dissolves the resin and carries it into the paver, giving a deeper, richer wet look and recoats that merge rather than stack. Water suspends acrylic particles that coalesce into a film on the surface, giving a much wider margin for error, low odour and easy clean-up.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For a first DIY sealing job, water-based is the right default and not a compromise — the appearance gap has narrowed a great deal and the forgiveness difference is large. Choose solvent when the look genuinely matters most, the site can disperse the fumes safely, and you have done it before. Whichever you pick, do not switch types without stripping, and write down what you used for next time.
          </p>
